      "Why site diaries matter for electricians: what to record daily, how it protects you legally, and a simple system that takes minutes, not hours, to run.",
    content: `
<p>Ask most electricians whether they keep a proper site diary and you'll get a shrug, or a vague answer about texting the customer if something comes up. A genuine <strong>site diary</strong> is one of the simplest habits in the trade, and one of the most consistently skipped — right up until a dispute, an insurance claim, or an EICR query makes you wish you'd written something down at the time.</p>

<h2>What This Actually Is</h2>
<p>A site diary is a dated, ongoing record of what happened on a job — work carried out, materials used, conditions found, instructions given or received, and anything unusual or unexpected. It's not the same as your certification paperwork; it's the day-to-day narrative that sits behind it, and it's often the single piece of evidence that settles a dispute months or years after the job's finished.</p>

<h2>Why It Matters More Than Most Electricians Assume</h2>
<p>At <a href="/">ECSPrep</a>, we think professional habits like this deserve as much attention as exam preparation itself.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Why a Site Diary Helps</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Client disputes scope of work</td><td>Dated record shows exactly what was agreed and completed each day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insurance or liability claim</td><td>Contemporaneous notes carry far more weight than memory recalled later</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unexpected finding mid-job</td><td>Written record of conditions found protects you if it's queried afterwards</td></tr>
<tr><td>Variation to original agreed work</td><td>Diary entry showing instruction received supports any additional charge</td></tr>
<tr><td>Future EICR or inspection query</td><td>Historical record helps explain decisions made at the time of installation</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>What to Actually Record Each Day</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Date and site details.</strong> Basic but essential — a diary with no dates is far less useful as evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Work carried out.</strong> A brief, factual summary of what was done, not a full technical narrative.</li>
<li><strong>Materials and circuits worked on.</strong> Enough detail to reconstruct what happened if it's ever queried later.</li>
<li><strong>Conditions found.</strong> Anything unexpected, damaged, or non-compliant discovered during the work.</li>
<li><strong>Instructions given or received.</strong> Any verbal agreement with the client about scope changes, deserves a written note the same day.</li>
<li><strong>Anyone else on site.</strong> Other trades, the client, or anyone who might be relevant if the day is ever queried.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A Simple Habit That Actually Gets Used</h2>
<p>Good habits like this pair well with steady exam preparation — our <a href="/study-plan">personalised study plan</a> and <a href="/flashcards">flashcard revision tools</a> apply the same "little and often" principle to ECS revision.</p>
<p>The reason most electricians don't keep a site diary isn't that they don't understand its value — it's that anything complicated gets abandoned within a week. A genuinely workable system takes two or three minutes at the end of each working day: a dated note in a phone app, a simple notebook kept in the van, or a shared digital log if you're running a team. Consistency matters far more than format.</p>

<h2>How This Connects to Your Wider Paperwork</h2>
<p>A site diary isn't a replacement for proper certification — your <a href="/blog/inspection-testing-certificates-guide">EIC, EICR and Minor Works certificates</a> remain the formal record of the work itself. What the diary adds is context: why a decision was made, what the client was told, and what conditions actually looked like on the day, none of which fits neatly into a certificate but all of which can matter enormously if a job is ever questioned.</p>

<h2>Building the Habit Into Daily Practice</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Pick one simple tool and stick with it.</strong> A notes app, a physical notebook, or a basic spreadsheet all work — the best system is the one you'll actually keep using.</li>
<li><strong>Write entries the same day, not later.</strong> Details fade fast, and a diary written up a week later loses most of its evidential value.</li>
<li><strong>Keep entries factual and brief.</strong> A short, accurate note beats a long one written days later from memory.</li>
<li><strong>Back it up somewhere safe.</strong> A digital copy or photo of paper entries protects the record if a notebook goes missing.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Where This Fits Into Wider Professional Practice</h2>
<p>Good record keeping sits alongside proper toolbox talks, RAMS documentation, and accurate certification as part of running a genuinely professional operation. Our <a href="/blog/toolbox-talks-electricians-guide">toolbox talks guide</a> and <a href="/blog/rams-risk-assessment-method-statement-electricians-guide">RAMS explained guide</a> both cover related documentation habits worth building alongside a site diary. For general guidance on record keeping obligations relevant to self-employed trades, <a href="https://www.gov.uk/self-employed-records" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GOV.UK's self-employed records guidance</a> is a useful starting reference. If you're still working toward your ECS card and building good professional habits from the start, <a href="/">ECSPrep</a>'s <a href="/blog/how-to-pass-ecs-test-first-time">study guide</a> covers the exam side of getting established properly in the trade.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>What's the difference between a site diary and a certificate?</h3>
<p>A certificate is the formal record of completed work meeting BS 7671 requirements, while a site diary is the day-to-day narrative of decisions, instructions and conditions behind it.</p>
<h3>How detailed does a site diary entry need to be?</h3>
<p>Brief and factual is enough — dates, work carried out, materials used, and any unusual findings or instructions, rather than a full technical write-up.</p>
<h3>Why does a site diary matter for disputes?</h3>
<p>A dated, contemporaneous record carries far more weight than memory recalled months later, and often settles disagreements about scope or instructions given.</p>
<h3>Should verbal instructions from a client be written down?</h3>
<p>Yes, any agreed change to scope given verbally should get a same-day written note, since this is exactly the kind of detail disputes tend to hinge on.</p>
<h3>Does a site diary help with insurance claims?</h3>
<p>Yes, contemporaneous notes about conditions found and work carried out are generally far more useful to an insurer than recollection after the fact.</p>
<h3>What's the easiest way to actually keep a site diary consistently?</h3>
<p>A simple, low-effort system — a phone notes app or a notebook kept in the van — used every single day beats a more detailed system that gets abandoned within weeks.</p>
<h3>Should a site diary be written at the end of each day?</h3>
<p>Yes, writing entries the same day preserves accuracy, since details fade quickly and a diary written up later loses much of its evidential value.</p>
<h3>Is a digital site diary better than a paper one?</h3>
<p>Either works well provided it's backed up safely and used consistently — the format matters far less than whether the habit actually sticks.</p>
`,
